cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "DyCelFEM: dynamic finite-element modelling of multiscale cell dynamics"
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite both the software itself and the
  paper it implements (listed under preferred-citation).
type: software

# Authors of the SOFTWARE. These are the two people who wrote the code, taken
# from the file headers; the paper below has five authors.
authors:
  - family-names: Zhao
    given-names: Jieling
  - family-names: Cao
    given-names: Youfang
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-5880-0006"

repository-code: "https://github.com/youfangcao/dycelfem"

# The concept DOI: always resolves to the newest release.
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.21985815"
identifiers:
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.21985815"
    description: "Concept DOI — resolves to the latest version"
  - type: doi
    value: "10.5281/zenodo.21985816"
    description: "DOI for version 1.0"

license: BSD-3-Clause
# Copyright is held by the University; see LICENSE.
copyright: "2026 The Board of Trustees of the University of Illinois"
version: "1.0"
date-released: "2026-08-11"
keywords:
  - wound healing
  - multiscale modelling
  - finite element method
  - agent-based model
  - systems biology
  - SBML

abstract: >-
  DyCelFEM simulates skin wound healing by coupling a deformable finite-element
  representation of individual cells to SBML-encoded intracellular reaction
  networks and inter-cell cytokine diffusion. Cell mechanics, growth, division,
  apoptosis and chemotactic migration are driven by the molecular state of each
  cell, allowing tissue-scale healing dynamics to emerge from subcellular
  signalling.

# The paper. Tools that support it will cite this in preference to the software
